WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.660
- During certain periods of my life,

00:00:01.660 --> 00:00:04.250
I have a very difficult time focusing

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:07.470
on pretty much anything
important or difficult.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:10.470
During these periods, it
seems almost impossible

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.660
to break out of the social media limbo,

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.350
where you're just constantly
switching between tabs,

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.760
refreshing pages, kind of waiting

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.220
for something interesting to happen,

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:23.400
like for someone to post a cool photo

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.820
on Instagram or something.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:27.120
You're kind of waiting to be entertained.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.280
But if you actually
have to apply yourself,

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.170
it's extremely difficult,
borderline painful, to do so.

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:34.730
And I'm pretty sure almost everyone

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:36.270
can relate to this problem.

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:38.910
I'm sure you've seen a
lot of videos on YouTube

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:40.400
giving you little tips and tricks

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.490
as to how to focus better,
including my own channel,

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:46.630
but there are very few
videos kind of diving in,

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:51.240
talking about why it's so
difficult to focus on hard things.

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:52.540
You know, like, what's the deal?

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.020
Why can't we just sit down

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.910
and do something important
with very little strain?

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:59.130
Why aren't we motivated to do the things

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:02.960
that would significantly
advance us further in our lives?

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.530
Because it's not like we
don't want those things.

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:06.363
It's just hard.

00:01:06.363 --> 00:01:10.410
So in this video, I want to
dive into why we procrastinate,

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:13.120
why it's so hard to do important things,

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:15.120
because knowing how this all works

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.080
helps us carve out a long-term solution.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:21.000
It can help us permanently
solve this problem.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.160
And that may sound really ambitious,

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:25.280
but I do believe it is possible.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.910
But, first we kind of have to discover

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:30.000
a little bit more about our human brain.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.990
So, weird thing about our
human brain, our human brain;

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:34.910
I'm really trying to
convince you that I'm human;

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:36.650
the weird thing about the brain is that

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:39.930
it pretty much hasn't changed
for thousands of years.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:41.710
We share, for the most part,

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:45.370
the exact same brain as our
hunter-gatherer ancestors.

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:46.650
And this is the type of brain

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:49.590
that was evolved to scan vast valleys

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:52.700
for the prospect of food or a fresh kill,

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:55.370
and absolutely light up and get excited

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:57.110
at the sight of an antelope,

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:59.030
so you can gather your friends together

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:00.270
and run after it, kill it.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:01.980
This is the kind of brain that,

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:05.040
as you're kind of going
through a berry bush

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.280
and you find a juicy berry,
you're super excited.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.640
You eat the berry and it
pretty much makes your day.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:12.600
You know, it's not really exciting stuff.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:14.650
There wasn't a lot of
stuff to do back then,

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:16.700
but that's just kind of the way it was.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:18.670
And the reason why we were motivated

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:22.150
to go run after an antelope
or eat a fresh berry

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:25.070
is the same reason,
wow, is the same reason

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:28.580
we're motivated to go on
Facebook and, you know,

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:31.540
check our notifications,
go on Instagram, et cetera.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:33.990
The reason why we're motivated
to do any of this stuff

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:36.820
is because of a brain
chemical called dopamine.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:39.290
Now, you've probably seen
a lot of videos on YouTube,

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:42.080
if you're into self-improvement,
about dopamine,

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.280
but dopamine is kind of misunderstood,

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:47.410
because most people think
it's the feel-good chemical.

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:49.290
It's a brain chemical that's released

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.000
when you're experiencing pleasure.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.130
It's the reason why you
experience pleasure,

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:54.510
but that's not exactly true,

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:57.610
because dopamine is the
motivating brain chemical.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:00.370
Dopamine is what's
released when you feel like

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:02.720
you're about to be rewarded,
or when you feel like

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:05.010
you're about to experience
something pleasurable.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:08.060
When your ancient ancestors
saw the antelope frolic

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:10.610
across the field, they
got a squirt of dopamine,

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:12.530
which motivated them to go kill it.

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:15.620
When you see a notification
pop up on Facebook,

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:17.040
you get a squirt of dopamine,

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:18.540
which motivates you to click it.

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:20.820
And what this means is that dopamine

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:23.033
is what controls your motivation.

00:03:23.033 --> 00:03:24.145
- Thank you, boys.

00:03:24.145 --> 00:03:25.730
Oh, did you get a big McChicken?

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:26.920
- [Man] Yeah, you said McChicken.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:28.160
- Now, why is this important?

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:31.420
Despite the fact that we
share the exact same brains

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:33.680
as our hunter-gatherer ancestors,

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.650
we don't live in that world anymore.

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:38.350
We live in a world of
super normal stimuli,

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:40.980
through ferocious
technological advancement

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:44.630
and capitalistic ventures, or
whatever you want to call it.

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:46.710
We live in a world where
things are designed

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:49.420
to be as pleasurable as humanly possible.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:51.780
And what super normal stimuli does is

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:55.400
it hijacks our natural reward pathways.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:57.370
It tricks our brain into thinking

00:03:57.370 --> 00:04:01.140
we are about to do something
so important for our survival

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.810
that we have an almost irresistible urge

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:05.390
towards doing that thing.

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:07.530
A good example of this is junk food.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:10.100
Food is integral for our survival,

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:12.470
and high-calorie foods are usually

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:14.270
even more important for our survival

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:16.730
because, you know, they'll
keep us alive longer.

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:19.690
The more calories, the longer
we'll live, roughly speaking.

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:23.110
See, what junk food does is
it's mechanically engineered

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:26.630
to be as high-fat,
high-calorie as possible.

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:28.800
So, when we get that beautiful,

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:30.700
when we eat that beautiful potato chip,

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:32.200
our brain remembers that.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:34.790
And the next time we see
a bag of potato chips

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:38.240
at the grocery store, there's
a huge amount of dopamine

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:42.250
that's released, motivating
you to go grab that reward,

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:44.610
that reward that's
integral for your survival.

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:47.430
So, we almost become slaves
to our dopamine system.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:50.910
We almost just go where our
dopamine system tells us to go.

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:54.370
That's why we get into
kind of social media binge

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:57.160
because our brain is telling us to stay

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:59.000
on these social media sites,

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.400
because there's a chance
that we might get a reward.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:03.460
We might get social validation,

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:05.670
or we might be able to look at something

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:07.280
kind of stimulating and naughty.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:09.680
It's the dopamine system that's telling us

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:11.660
and motivating us to stay there.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:12.640
So, what does this all mean

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:14.160
if we're bringing this all together?

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:17.470
Basically, for most of us
who have difficulty focusing

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:19.740
and we find it really, really
difficult to be motivated

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:22.990
to do harder things, that is only because

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:25.170
we have reprogrammed our brain

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:27.790
to fall for this super normal stimuli

00:05:27.790 --> 00:05:31.070
rather than normal
stimuli, natural stimuli,

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:33.580
because it's not that
these natural rewards

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:36.330
aren't rewarding, they're
just less rewarding

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:39.440
than the artificial super normal rewards.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:41.980
So, the long-term solution
to be able to focus

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:45.880
on boring things, is
to reprogram our brain

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:48.800
to remember that these things
are actually pleasurable,

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:51.610
and really good for us, and
integral for our survival.

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:54.360
And this isn't just about a dopamine fast

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:56.600
for a couple of days
or something like that.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:06:00.520
We almost need to reset
our baseline long-term.

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:02.850
We need to think about
how much stimulation

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:04.610
we want in our lives.

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:06.470
What kind of super normal stimulus

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:08.120
do we want to take part in?

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:09.790
You know, I've noticed in my own life

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:12.450
that when I'm camping for
a long period of time,

00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:15.140
and there isn't much to
do other than figure out

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:18.430
how to cook a fish over a
fire, or set up my tent,

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:20.350
or go for a walk in the woods,

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:22.530
or go swimming, or lay on the sand,

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:24.900
in these situations, little things

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:28.020
like having a conversation
with a sibling or a friend

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.720
becomes immensely pleasurable.

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:31.340
It's the same reason why people

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:33.590
who break out of an addiction cycle,

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:36.500
they stop doing their
immensely pleasurable thing

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:39.250
cold turkey, after a
certain amount of time,

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:42.580
they find an increased
enjoyment out of life itself.

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:45.470
Pretty much everything in
life becomes more enjoyable.

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:47.110
They start to find motivation

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:49.150
to do things other than their addiction.

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:50.980
You know, it's something
that I also noticed

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:53.540
during the quarantine,
why it was so hard for me

00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:56.020
to find my work ethic again.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:58.130
It's because all that time inside

00:06:58.130 --> 00:06:59.750
made me an Internet addict.

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:02.810
I spent so much time looking at screens

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:05.890
that my baseline of
dopamine and stimulation

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:08.920
was so high that the more boring things

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:12.130
became almost painful to do in comparison.

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:15.430
The disparity of
stimulation became so vast

00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:19.660
that I felt physical pain
trying to do harder things.

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:21.490
So, if we're able to find a way

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:23.570
to genuinely enjoy the things

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:25.313
that make our lives better,

00:07:26.290 --> 00:07:27.440
then what are we doing?

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:29.180
Like, why don't we just go and do that?

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:30.640
I really want to challenge you guys

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:33.760
over the next couple of weeks or months

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:36.370
to really think about how
much stimulation you want

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:38.290
to invite into your life.

00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:40.900
You know, what's actually
adding value to your life?

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:44.760
And can you reduce the amount
of super normal stimuli

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:47.310
that you're indulging in on a daily basis?

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:49.350
You may actually find that by reducing

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:52.730
your amount of time on
social media or on YouTube,

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:55.440
or eating junk food, or you name it,

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:59.000
you may actually come to
genuinely look forward

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:00.840
to socializing.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:03.800
You might actually look
forward to reading a book.

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:06.580
You might actually be
motivated to go to the gym.

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:09.180
There will be that dopamine driver

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:10.930
